# Resource limits
#
# Every countable table in the compiler and runtime has a hard cap, and
# the compiler / VM refuse to silently truncate past it. Every limit
# produces the same error shape:
#
#   <limit-name>: limit exceeded (N > CAP) in function `foo` at file:line:col. <fix hint>
#
# Editors and agents can grep for the limit name and the `file:line:col`
# segment to jump to the offending expression.
#
# When you hit one of these, read the fix hint — it's concrete. If the
# error says "hoist subexpressions into `let` bindings", that is the
# fix; the compiler is telling you the function's peak live-register
# pressure is too high for a single u8 register file, and factoring the
# expression into named locals drops the peak.
#
# The same limits apply whether you `fai run` (VM interpreter) or build
# to WebAssembly (`fai build --target wasm-html`, `fai run --wasm`).
def doc_limits_overview
    @return Void
do
  # The full inventory — see the per-section entries below for the fix
  # patterns.
  #
  #   registers               255 per function
  #   parameters              255 per function
  #   upvalues                255 per closure
  #   call arguments          255 per call
  #   string pool             65535 per module
  #   constants               65535 per function
  #   wasm native method ids  255 per module (WASM backend only)
  #   call depth              1024 runtime frames
end

# registers — 255 per function.
#
# Each let-binding, each temporary for a subexpression, each function
# argument lands in a register. The VM and WASM codegen both address
# registers via u8 indices, so the peak live-register count in a single
# function can't exceed 255.
#
# Tripped by wide expressions — a type constructor called with 10+
# labelled args where each arg is a nested `unwrap(getString(d, '...'),
# '')` chain is the classic offender. Each nested call holds a live
# register for the outer call's arg slot PLUS registers for its own
# intermediates; at ~10 fields the peak blows through 255.
#
# Fix:
#   - Hoist each subexpression into a `let` so the compiler can reuse
#     the register slot once the let-binding isn't read again.
#   - Split the function into smaller helpers — one helper per 4-5
#     fields, then a composer that calls each and stitches the result.
def doc_limits_registers
    @return Void
do
  # Before (trips the cap):
  #   MyType(
  #     a: unwrap(getString(d, 'a'), ''),
  #     b: unwrap(getString(d, 'b'), ''),
  #     ...  # 10+ of these
  #   )
  #
  # After (compiles cleanly, same behaviour):
  #   let a = unwrap(getString(d, 'a'), '')
  #   let b = unwrap(getString(d, 'b'), '')
  #   ...
  #   MyType(a: a, b: b, ...)
end

# parameters — 255 per function.
#
# `@param` entries. A function that takes 256+ params can't be called
# because the call instruction indexes args via u8.
#
# Fix: collect related params into a typed record and pass one value.
def doc_limits_parameters
    @return Void
do
  # Before:
  #   def render
  #     @param a1 Int @param a2 Int ... @param a260 Int
  #     @return View
  #   do ... end
  #
  # After:
  #   type Config a1 Int; a2 Int; ... end
  #   def render
  #     @param c Config
  #     @return View
  #   do ... end
end

# upvalues — 255 per closure.
#
# A closure captures variables from enclosing scopes as upvalues. The
# upvalue index is u8, so a single closure can't capture 256+ distinct
# values.
#
# Fix: capture one record that bundles the needed fields, instead of
# capturing them individually.
def doc_limits_upvalues
    @return Void
do
  # Before:
  #   def makeHandler
  #     @return Handler
  #   do
  #     let a1 = ...; let a2 = ...; ... # 260 locals captured individually
  #     do; useAll(a1, a2, ...) end
  #   end
  #
  # After — bundle into one record so the closure captures a single
  # upvalue:
  #   type HandlerCtx a1 Int; a2 Int; ... end
  #   def makeHandler
  #     @return Handler
  #   do
  #     let ctx = HandlerCtx(a1: ..., a2: ...)
  #     do; useAll(ctx) end
  #   end
end

# call arguments — 255 per call.
#
# `foo(a1, a2, ..., a260)` — the call opcode stores arg_count as u8.
#
# Fix: pack the arguments into a Dictionary or Array and unpack inside
# the function.
def doc_limits_call_arguments
    @return Void
do
  # Before:
  #   foo(a1, a2, ..., a260)
  #
  # After:
  #   foo({ 'a1': a1, 'a2': a2, ... })
  #   # and inside `foo`, read fields back via getInt(args, 'a1') etc.
end

# string pool — 65535 per module.
#
# Each distinct string literal lives in the module-level string pool,
# indexed via u16.
#
# This cap is large enough that real code shouldn't hit it — if you do,
# the module has grown past what's sustainable. Split by topic, or
# move static tables into a dedicated data module.
def doc_limits_string_pool
    @return Void
do
  # If you're auto-generating source, deduplicate repeated literals
  # before emitting — the pool already dedupes, but the parser still
  # walks every occurrence.
end

# constants — 65535 per function.
#
# Each distinct Float / Int literal in a function lives in that
# function's constant pool, indexed via u16.
#
# Same story as string pool: hitting this cap means the function has
# grown past what's maintainable. Split into helpers.
def doc_limits_constants
    @return Void
do
  # Before:
  #   def massive ... do
  #     if x == 1.1; ... elsif x == 1.2; ... # 65k+ of these
  #   end
  #
  # After: rewrite the branching as a table lookup.
  #   let branches = [1.1, 1.2, ...]
end

# wasm native method ids — 255 per module (WASM backend only).
#
# The WASM codegen dispatches std-library methods via a u8 method_id
# switch in the runtime. This cap is a ceiling on how many distinct
# native methods the runtime exposes — it's a language-implementation
# limit, not a user-facing one. If you hit it while adding a new std
# method, reuse a dispatch slot or widen the method_id encoding.
def doc_limits_wasm_native_method_ids
    @return Void
do
  # You only see this if you're extending the WASM codegen. Normal
  # forai code never hits it.
end

# call depth — 1024 runtime frames.
#
# Unbounded recursion would blow the host thread's stack (Rust native
# or wasmtime). The VM caps call depth at 1024 and surfaces a clean
# `call depth: limit exceeded ...` runtime error instead of a process
# abort.
#
# Fix: rewrite recursion as iteration, or use an accumulator to make
# the recursion shallower. Tail-call optimisation is not guaranteed.
def doc_limits_call_depth
    @return Void
do
  # Before (blows the cap at n > 1024):
  #   def sum
  #     @param n Int
  #     @return Int
  #   do
  #     if n <= 0; 0 else; n + sum(n - 1) end
  #   end
  #
  # After (iterative; unbounded):
  #   def sum
  #     @param n Int
  #     @return Int
  #   do
  #     var total = 0
  #     var i = n
  #     while i > 0
  #       total = total + i
  #       i = i - 1
  #     end
  #     total
  #   end
end

# Backend parity.
#
# Every limit above applies to both the VM interpreter (`fai run`) and
# the WASM backend (`fai run --wasm`, `fai build --target wasm-html`).
# If a program compiles on one and not the other, that is a bug — file
# an issue with the offending source.
#
# The one exception is `wasm native method ids`, which is an internal
# limit of the WASM codegen's dispatch table and never surfaces in user
# programs.
def doc_limits_backend_parity
    @return Void
do
  # Compile-time limits are enforced in the shared compiler pipeline,
  # so they surface identically on both backends.
  # Runtime limits (call depth) are enforced by each backend's VM and
  # use the same error shape.
end
